\title{iconic: Causal Model Selection with Genetic Instruments and Negative Controls}
\description{
Provides a model selection workflow for causal inference with genetic instruments and negative controls in observational omics data. The workflow auto-trains a hybrid generative texture model from the user's iconic_data object: a torch GAN learns the sample-level covariate, exposure, and outcome structure, while a Gaussian copula model learns the full feature-level joint distribution of the mediator panel (marginal distributions via empirical CDF with parametric fallback, cross-feature dependence via Gaussian copula). This ensures synthetic mediator texture preserves realistic marginal shapes (skewness, tails, bounded support) and cross-feature correlations from the user's cohort. The negative-control panel remains generated by a pluggable nc_model with residual correlation injection. The torch package is required (no multivariate-normal fallback). The infer_confounding() function estimates held-fixed confounding parameters (confounding strength, mediator-outcome confounding, NC coverage, number of latent confounders) from the data via estimator-gap and parallel-analysis methods, with a confounding = "inferred" option in iconic_sensitivity() and iconic_prospect(). The modular API: iconic_data() constructs a standardized data object; iconic_diagnose() runs instrument-strength checks (partial F-statistics), negative-control validity screens (A1: W-perp-Z-given-C, A2: W-perp-G-given-C, A2': W-perp-Gm-given-C with BH-FDR control), and path-completeness assessment; iconic_estimate() fits all eligible estimators (UNADJ, DIRECT, COCA, IV2SLS, PGC, IV2SLS2, PGC2, PGC2Gm); iconic_sensitivity() generates a degradation surface over instrument-independence violations (rho_G1 x rho_G2 grid) calibrated to the user's data; and iconic_recommend() ranks estimators by identification strength (tier A: identified, B: negative-control, C: instrument-based, D: bias reference) and robustness. For users without instruments or negative controls, iconic_prospect() performs prospective analysis showing how estimates would change if such data were collected. The package includes publication figure plotting functions (ggplot2 + patchwork) that reproduce all manuscript figures from package computation results. The package retains its full simulation benchmarking infrastructure, including mediation estimators for natural direct (NDE) and natural indirect (NIE) effects under mediator-outcome confounding, a weak-instrument guard (Stock & Yogo, 2005), pleiotropy sensitivity sweeps, and empirical negative-control validity diagnostics. The data-generating process supports imperfect instrument independence (rho_G1, rho_G2, rho_pop, separate_U, omega_1, omega_2) and a tunable instrument strength parameter (gamma_G). The data-generating process also supports block-diagonal feature correlations (feat_cor) modelling co-expression modules, sweepable via sweep_mediation_param("feat_cor", ...) with a dedicated plot_feature_correlation_sweep() figure function. All computationally intensive functions (iconic_estimate, iconic_sensitivity, iconic_prospect, iconic_diagnose, infer_confounding, sweep_nc_validity, sweep_instrument_strength, and the exported analysis functions) support multi-core parallelization via the n_cores argument, using parallel::mclapply on Unix and PSOCK clusters on Windows, with message()-based progress milestones.
